DEEP[1]=('Chapter 17 is named Śraddhā-Traya-Vibhāga Yoga, the yoga of the three kinds of faith. It follows directly from 16.23–24, where Kṛṣṇa made scripture the guide. Arjuna immediately thinks of the many sincere people who never read scripture at all.',
 'Śaṅkara understands Arjuna to mean people who do not know the scriptural rules, not those who reject them. Rāmānuja adds that the question is about the worth of faith itself. Kṛṣṇa does not answer with “good” or “bad”. He says faith takes on the colour of the person’s nature, and then spends the rest of the chapter showing how to tell the colours apart. Many modern readers see this as one of the most humane passages in the Gītā, because it takes the ordinary believer seriously.',
 'Your sincerity matters. So does the quality of what you trust. Look at both.')
DEEP[2]=('The guṇas were explained in chapter 14 as forces in the mind. Here Kṛṣṇa turns them into a practical test that can be applied to anything people do. The chapter will apply it to faith, worship, food, sacrifice, austerity and giving.',
 'Commentators note the phrase <i>svabhāva-jā</i>, born of one’s own nature. Faith is not chosen in a vacuum; it grows out of temperament and past habits (<i>saṃskāras</i>). Śaṅkara says the nature formed by past lives decides the kind of faith one has now. Rāmānuja says the guṇa that dominates in a person colours his faith. This is hopeful as well as sobering: since nature can be changed by food, company and practice, faith can be refined.',
 'What you trust reflects who you are, and who you are can grow. Start with small daily choices.')
DEEP[3]=('This verse is one of the most quoted in the Gītā. It says that a person is not only what he thinks, but what he deeply trusts. The same idea appears in the Chāndogya Upaniṣad (3.14.1): <i>kratumayaḥ puruṣaḥ</i>, a person is made of resolve; as one resolves in this world, so one becomes hereafter.',
 'Here <i>sattva</i> is read by Śaṅkara as the mind or inner being, not the guṇa. Rāmānuja reads it as the inner organ shaped by past impressions. Vivekananda often quoted the line to young people. His point was that people become what they believe themselves to be, so they should believe in their strength. Modern psychology speaks of self-fulfilling beliefs and mindsets in a similar spirit.',
 'Notice what you most deeply trust in. Without noticing, you are becoming it.')
DEEP[4]=('<i>Yakṣas</i> are nature spirits associated with wealth; Kubera is their king. <i>Rākṣasas</i> are beings of force and violence, such as Rāvaṇa in the Rāmāyaṇa. <i>Pretas</i> are restless spirits of the dead, and <i>bhūtas</i> are elemental spirits.',
 'Commentators explain that the object of worship mirrors the worshipper’s desires. Those who want clarity and goodness turn to the gods, the shining powers. Those driven by ambition worship power and wealth. Those ruled by fear and darkness turn to ghosts and spirits, often in the hope of harming others or protecting themselves by magic. Śaṅkara notes that even within one tradition, people can worship the same deity with very different motives. The verse invites honest self-examination rather than judging others.',
 'Look at what you most admire or pray for. It tells you a great deal about yourself.')
DEEP[5]=('Ancient India had many kinds of extreme ascetics: some stood on one leg for years, some lay on beds of thorns, some fasted to starvation. The epics also tell of demons like Rāvaṇa and Hiraṇyakaśipu who did fierce austerities to win powers they later misused.',
 'The Gītā consistently rejects self-torture. In 6.16–17 it praises moderation in food and sleep. The Buddha, after years of extreme fasting, also rejected it and taught the middle way. Commentators note the motives named here: hypocrisy, ego, desire, attachment and the pride of strength. Śaṅkara says such austerity, done against scripture and from ego, is demonic in nature. The sentence continues into the next verse, which gives the reason.',
 'Discipline should make you stronger and kinder. If it only makes you proud or ill, it has gone wrong.')
DEEP[6]=('The body, in the Gītā’s view, is a city where the Lord dwells (5.13, 13.22, 15.15). To torture it is therefore to torment the Lord within.',
 'Commentators are struck by this teaching. Śaṅkara says such ascetics trouble the body made of the elements, and also the Lord who witnesses within. Rāmānuja says they disregard the Lord’s command to care for the body as an instrument of worship. Many later teachers said the same. The Tirumantiram of Tirumūlar says that by caring for the body he cared for the soul, since the body is the temple of God. This is not a licence for indulgence, but a call to respect.',
 'Care for your body as you would for a temple. The Divine lives there.')
DEEP[7]=('After faith and worship, Kṛṣṇa turns to the most ordinary act of daily life: eating. Then come sacrifice, austerity and giving, the three religious duties praised in 18.5.',
 'Commentators enjoy that food begins the list. Indian tradition has always linked food and mind. The Chāndogya Upaniṣad (6.5.4) says the subtlest part of food becomes mind, and (7.26.2) that when food is pure, the mind becomes pure. Śaṅkara says the foods one likes reveal one’s guṇa. Āyurveda developed this into detailed guidance. The teaching works both ways: what we like shows who we are, and what we eat shapes who we become.',
 'Look at your plate today as part of your inner life, not separate from it.')
DEEP[8]=('Sāttvika food, as described in later Āyurvedic and yogic texts, includes fresh grains, fruits, vegetables, milk, ghee, nuts and honey, prepared simply and eaten in moderation.',
 'Notice the positive tone of this verse: tasty, pleasing, substantial food is sāttvika. Commentators stress that the Gītā does not ask for bland or joyless eating. The six qualities named, increasing life, purity, strength, health, happiness and cheerfulness, are effects to watch for after a meal. Śaṅkara explains “smooth” as containing natural oils, and “substantial” as nourishing for a long time. The verse is also the basis of much traditional temple cooking, offered first to the deity as <i>prasāda</i>.',
 'After a meal, notice: do I feel lighter, clearer, more cheerful? That is a good sign.')
DEEP[9]=('The six tastes of Āyurveda are sweet, sour, salty, pungent, bitter and astringent. All have a place; this verse warns against excess of the sharp ones.',
 'The key word is <i>ati</i>, “too”: too bitter, too sour, too hot. Commentators note that such foods excite rajas: restlessness, irritability and craving. Śaṅkara links them to pain and disease. Modern readers often notice the link between very spicy, salty or stimulating diets and a restless mind. The point is moderation, not banning spices, which Indian cooking uses wisely for digestion and health.',
 'Go easy on the very spicy, salty and stimulating. Your mind may calm along with your stomach.')
DEEP[10]=('<i>Yātayāma</i> literally means food that has passed through a <i>yāma</i>, a watch of about three hours. Traditional households avoided eating food cooked many hours earlier.',
 'Commentators also include food that is spoiled, smelly, or touched by impurity, and the leftovers of others’ plates. Śaṅkara adds food that has lost its essence. In the age before refrigeration, stale food was a real health risk, and the verse reflects that wisdom. Today many readers take it as a call to fresh, home-cooked meals. The mental effect described, dullness and heaviness, is the mark of tamas.',
 'Freshness matters. Food that has lost its life tends to make a heavy mind.')
DEEP[11]=('<i>Yajña</i> here means worship and offering in the broad sense of 4.25–33, as well as Vedic ritual.',
 'The heart of this verse is the phrase <i>yaṣṭavyam eva</i>, “it ought to be done”. Commentators see karma yoga applied to worship: offering because it is right, not to get something. Śaṅkara says such worship purifies the mind. Rāmānuja says it pleases the Lord. Kant, the European philosopher, praised acting from duty rather than inclination; the Gītā’s ideal is similar, but warmed by devotion.',
 'Do good because it is good. That motive needs no applause to keep going.')
DEEP[12]=('The Mahābhārata tells of Yudhiṣṭhira’s grand horse sacrifice after the war. A mongoose with half its body turned golden arrived and said the sacrifice was not equal to a poor family’s gift of a little barley flour to a hungry guest. That gift had turned half of it gold.',
 'Commentators point to this story (Āśvamedhika Parva) to explain rājasika sacrifice. Two motives spoil an offering: the desire for reward and the desire to be seen. Śaṅkara says the outer act may be perfect, yet the fruit is limited. Rāmānuja says such worship binds one to its rewards. The size of a ritual means little; its spirit means everything.',
 'Before a good deed, ask quietly: for reward, for show, or for love?')
DEEP[13]=('A Vedic sacrifice traditionally included distributing food to all present and giving a fee, <i>dakṣiṇā</i>, to the priests. Without these, the rite was considered incomplete.',
 'Commentators note that “no food distributed” is listed among the faults. A true sacrifice feeds others; generosity is built into worship. Śaṅkara says a sacrifice without faith, mantras and gifts is tāmasika. Rāmānuja agrees. Many Hindu festivals still end with a shared meal, <i>annadāna</i>, the gift of food. Sikh <i>laṅgar</i> carries a similar spirit of feeding all who come.',
 'Worship that does not reach others in generosity is incomplete. Share something today.')
DEEP[14]=('Verses 14–16 redefine <i>tapas</i>, austerity, in gentle and practical terms: body, speech and mind. This threefold division of action runs through Indian ethics, Hindu, Buddhist and Jain.',
 'Commentators note how different this is from the harsh austerities of 17.5–6. Bodily austerity here means respect for gods, teachers and the wise; cleanliness; uprightness; restraint; and non-violence. Śaṅkara explains “twice-born” as the learned. Rāmānuja reads <i>brahmacarya</i> as restraint of the senses. Gandhi took this verse as a guide for daily discipline in his ashram.',
 'Respect, cleanliness and non-violence are already a full discipline. Practise them today.')
DEEP[15]=('The Manusmṛti (4.138) gives a famous rule: <i>satyaṃ brūyāt priyaṃ brūyāt, na brūyāt satyam apriyam</i>, “speak the truth, speak it pleasantly; do not speak an unpleasant truth harshly”.',
 'Commentators note that all four qualities must be present together: speech should not cause distress, and should be true, pleasant and beneficial. Śaṅkara says if even one is missing, it is not austerity of speech. Buddhist teaching on right speech is very close: true, kind, timely and helpful. The verse adds <i>svādhyāya</i>, regular recitation of scripture, as part of disciplined speech: using the voice for the sacred.',
 'Before speaking, ask: is it true, kind and useful? If not, silence is better.')
DEEP[16]=('Body, speech and mind: the three channels of action, <i>trikaraṇa</i>. Many Indian prayers ask forgiveness for wrongs done “by body, speech or mind”.',
 'Commentators call mental austerity the subtlest and most important, because body and speech follow the mind. <i>Manaḥ-prasāda</i>, serenity of mind, recalls 2.64–65. <i>Saumyatva</i>, gentleness, is the mood of the moon. <i>Mauna</i>, silence, is read by Śaṅkara as control of the mind that makes speech restrained. <i>Bhāva-saṃśuddhi</i> is purity of motive, the opposite of hidden agendas.',
 'A calm and gentle mind is the finest discipline. Begin each day with a few quiet minutes.')
DEEP[17]=('The three kinds of austerity, of body, speech and mind, can each be done in a sāttvika, rājasika or tāmasika way.',
 'Commentators note that the difference lies in faith and motive, not in the outer practice. The same fast, the same silence, the same pilgrimage can be pure or proud. Śaṅkara says austerity done with supreme faith and without desire for reward is sāttvika. Rāmānuja says it is offered to the Lord. Many teachers say the test is simple: does the practice make me humbler and kinder?',
 'Practise your disciplines for their own sake, with trust. Results will look after themselves.')
DEEP[18]=('Austerity for show was well known in the Gītā’s time, and the Gītā is not alone in criticising it. The Gospel of Matthew (6.16) warns against fasting with a sad face to be seen by others.',
 'Commentators note the words <i>calam adhruvam</i>, unsteady and fleeting. Austerity for praise lasts only while the audience is watching. Śaṅkara says its fruit, honour and reverence, is itself passing. Rāmānuja says it binds one to the very praise it seeks. Social media offers new forms of this old temptation.',
 'Discipline for show fades when the audience leaves. Discipline for truth endures in private.')
DEEP[19]=('The epics tell of asuras who did terrible austerities to gain power over the gods: Hiraṇyakaśipu, Rāvaṇa, Bhasmāsura. Their tapas was real, but its aim was harm.',
 'Commentators list three marks of tāmasika austerity: foolish obstinacy, self-torture, and the aim of harming another. Śaṅkara mentions rites performed to destroy enemies by sorcery. Rāmānuja says such tapas springs from delusion. The verse warns that discipline itself is neutral; its direction decides its worth. Spite dressed up as spirituality is still spite.',
 'Self-punishment and spite are not spiritual. Let them go.')
DEEP[20]=('<i>Dāna</i>, giving, is one of the oldest Indian virtues. The Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad (5.2.3) tells how the thunder said “da, da, da” to gods, humans and demons: be self-controlled, give, be compassionate.',
 'Commentators unpack four conditions of sāttvika giving: as a duty, to one who cannot repay, at the right place and time, to a worthy person. Śaṅkara explains the right place as a holy place, and the right time as a sacred day. Many traditions agree on the core: give without bargaining. Hindu temples, Sikh laṅgars and Muslim zakāt all embody this spirit in different ways. Maimonides’ ladder of charity also ranks anonymous giving high.',
 'Give thoughtfully, to the right person, expecting nothing back. That is the purest gift.')
DEEP[21]=('A gift given “to get something back” may expect a return favour, praise, or merit in the next world.',
 'Commentators note that such giving is closer to trade than charity. Śaṅkara includes giving reluctantly, with a heavy heart. Rāmānuja says it binds one to its expected return. The Taittirīya Upaniṣad (1.11.3) gives rules for giving: with faith, not without faith; with grace, with modesty, with fear, with understanding. A grudging gift lacks every one of these.',
 'Give freely or not at all. A gift given with a sigh helps no one.')
DEEP[22]=('A gift given with contempt, or thrown at the receiver, was considered among the worst failings in Indian ethics.',
 'Commentators stress that the manner of giving is part of the gift. Śaṅkara says giving without respect, or with insults, is tāmasika. Giving to the unworthy, such as one who will misuse it to harm others, is also included. The point is care: generosity needs thought as well as warmth. A small gift given with respect honours both giver and receiver.',
 'Give with respect. The way you give is part of the gift.')
DEEP[23]=('<i>Oṃ tat sat</i> closes the colophon of every chapter of the Gītā, and many other Hindu texts and rites.',
 'Commentators explain each word in the following verses: Oṃ (17.24), tat (17.25) and sat (17.26–27). Together they name Brahman and are used to sanctify acts. Śaṅkara says even imperfect actions are made complete by uttering these words with faith. Rāmānuja says they link every act to the Supreme. The formula is short enough to say before any task, sacred or ordinary.',
 'A word of dedication can turn any task into an offering. Try one before your next piece of work.')
DEEP[24]=('Most Vedic hymns, mantras and prayers begin with Oṃ. The Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad is entirely about its meaning.',
 'Commentators say Oṃ is used at the start of acts to invoke Brahman and to remove defects. Śaṅkara says those who know Brahman always begin with it. Patañjali (Yoga Sūtra 1.27–28) calls Oṃ the word for the Lord and recommends its repetition with meaning. Today, Oṃ is chanted at the start of classes, meetings and prayers across India.',
 'Begin important tasks with a moment of dedication. A single breath and word can be enough.')
DEEP[25]=('<i>Tat</i>, “That”, points to Brahman without naming it, as in the Upaniṣadic saying <i>tat tvam asi</i>.',
 'Commentators explain that saying “tat” is like saying “not mine, but That’s”. It dedicates the action and its fruit to the Supreme. Śaṅkara says it is used by seekers of liberation who act without seeking reward. Rāmānuja says it reminds the doer that all belongs to the Lord. The custom of saying “Kṛṣṇārpaṇam astu” after actions has the same spirit.',
 'Say inwardly “this is for That”, and let the result go.')
DEEP[26]=('<i>Sat</i> means real and good at once. Sanskrit, like many languages, links what truly is and what is right.',
 'Commentators note the deep idea: what is real is good, and what is good shares in the real. <i>Satya</i>, truth, comes from the same root. Śaṅkara says <i>sat</i> is used for existence, goodness and praiseworthy deeds. Rāmānuja says good deeds connect us with the eternal. The Upaniṣadic prayer <i>asato mā sad gamaya</i>, “lead me from the unreal to the real”, uses the same word.',
 'Doing good aligns you with what is real. Let that give you confidence.')
DEEP[27]=('<i>Sthiti</i>, steadfastness, is a key word in the Gītā, from the <i>sthita-prajña</i> of 2.54 to Arjuna’s <i>sthito’smi</i>, “I stand firm”, in 18.73.',
 'Commentators note that consistency itself is called sat. One good act is good; a steady life of goodness is sacred. Śaṅkara says even actions done for the Lord’s sake, such as building a temple, are called sat. Rāmānuja says any act connected to worship becomes sat. The verse honours the quiet, faithful person who does good day after day without drama.',
 'Keep at it. Steadiness in goodness is itself holy.')
DEEP[28]=('<i>Asat</i>, the opposite of sat, means unreal or worthless. The chapter that began with faith (17.1) ends with faith.',
 'Commentators note the careful symmetry: Arjuna asked about those who worship with faith but outside the rules; Kṛṣṇa ends by saying that without faith, even correct acts are empty. Śaṅkara says acts without faith bring no fruit here or hereafter, so faith is essential. Rāmānuja says faith is what connects action to its true goal. The colophon names chapter 17 Śraddhā-Traya-Vibhāga Yoga.',
 'Bring trust and sincerity to what you do. Without them, even good acts feel hollow.')
